# Working with Sleekplan

Everything below applies **only when you are calling Sleekplan tools**. If a task
doesn't touch Sleekplan, ignore this rule entirely.

Sleekplan holds a team's customer feedback, their public roadmap, and their
public changelog. Writes are visible to real customers and some cannot be undone.

## Look up keys before you write

Feedback **types** and **statuses** are configured per workspace. They are not a
fixed vocabulary, and their keys are frequently opaque strings rather than
readable words. A key that exists in one workspace will not exist in the next.

Before any call that sets a type or status, call `list_feedback_types` /
`list_feedback_statuses` (or read `sleekplan://feedback-types` /
`sleekplan://feedback-statuses`) and pick a real `key` from the response. The
same holds for tags (`list_tags`), segments (`list_segments` — use the `slug`,
never `segment_id`), and owners (`list_admins`).

Never guess a key, never reuse a key you saw in another workspace, and never
carry one over from an earlier conversation.

## Two type registries, not one

Feedback types carry `disable_feedback` and `disable_changelog` flags, and in
most workspaces the two sets barely overlap:

- Creating or updating a **feedback post** → only types where `disable_feedback`
  is falsy.
- Creating or updating a **changelog entry** → only types where
  `disable_changelog` is falsy.

The type that looks obviously right for a release note is often the one that is
forbidden on changelog entries. Filter the list; don't pattern-match on the name.

## The roadmap is a status, not a separate object

There is no roadmap resource to write to. The public roadmap renders feedback
posts whose **status** has `roadmap: true`. To put something on the roadmap, move
the post to such a status with `update_feedback`. To take it off, move it to one
where `roadmap` is falsy. Read `list_feedback_statuses` to see which is which.

## Reading results

- Empty results surface as an **error** ("0 items found"), not an empty list.
  Treat that as "nothing matched" and move on — do not retry, and do not report
  it to the user as a failure.
- `advanced` on `list_feedback` takes a **stringified** JSON object, not an
  object.
- `estimated` on `update_feedback` must be `"{Month}, {YYYY}"` — full English
  month name, comma, single space, four-digit year (`"March, 2026"`). Anything
  else is rejected.
- `effort` is internal team precedence, `0`–`3`, unrelated to how users voted.
- `create_feedback` ignores owner, effort and estimated. Set those with a
  follow-up `update_feedback`.
- Comments you create are stamped as agent-authored, so the team can tell them
  apart from human replies later.

## Confirm before writing

Match the write to the right gate. When in doubt, use the stricter one.

| Gate | Applies to |
|---|---|
| **Just do it** | Every read. Drafting text into the chat or a local file. |
| **Confirm once, then batch** | Internal-only writes: tags, `effort`, owner, type. Show the full list of intended changes, get one yes, then proceed. |
| **Confirm each** | Anything a customer sees or that cannot be undone: publishing or updating a changelog entry, `notify`, `announcement`, status moves that change the public roadmap, `merge_feedback`, and any comment. |
| **Only when asked outright** | `delete_feedback`, `delete_changelog`, `delete_tag`. Never propose a delete as a cleanup step. |

Two defaults that keep the blast radius small:

- Create changelog entries with `draft: true` unless the user explicitly asked to
  publish. Let them publish from Sleekplan once they've read it.
- `merge_feedback` is one-way: the source post is closed into the target. Confirm
  the direction, not just the pair.

## Don't speak for the team

When writing anything customers will read — a comment, a changelog entry, a
status change — stick to what has actually happened in the repo. Do not promise
a delivery date, commit to a feature, or apologise on the team's behalf. If the
user wants a date published, ask them for it rather than inferring one from a
branch or a milestone.
